The English harness's test sets, made by ../make_sets.py from openly licensed text. None of it is the user's own
words or learned data. Each file keeps its sources' licences:

- next-chat.tsv, and the chat sentences in clean.tsv and phone-typos.tsv: prompts from OpenAssistant's oasst1
  (https://huggingface.co/datasets/OpenAssistant/oasst1, 2023-04-12 release), Apache License 2.0.
- next-news.tsv, and the news sentences in clean.tsv and phone-typos.tsv: English Wikinews articles
  (https://en.wikinews.org, the last 200 published; wikinews-articles.tsv lists each one's revision), Creative
  Commons Attribution 2.5, by Wikinews's contributors.
- misspellings.tsv: Wikipedia's "Lists of common misspellings/For machines" (revision 1199637275), Creative Commons
  Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0, by Wikipedia's contributors; this file is under the same licence.
- next-texts.tsv and text-typos.tsv: text messages from the NUS SMS Corpus, 2015-03-09 release (Tao Chen and
  Min-Yen Kan, "Creating a Live, Public Short Message Service Corpus: The NUS SMS Corpus", Language Resources and
  Evaluation 47(2), 2013; https://scholarbank.nus.edu.sg/handle/10635/137343, files from
  https://github.com/kite1988/nus-sms-corpus at bf0a3651), Creative Commons Attribution 4.0, by the National
  University of Singapore. nus-sms-held-out.txt lists their message ids. Messages with a name, a number or an
  address are left out (../make_sets.py says how).
- The touch points and typos in every file are generated (MIT, ../../../../LICENSE-SebType).
